    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for the USMLE and I'm a bit stuck on the RAAS pathway. Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors, like lisinopril? Specifically, how do they interact with the system to lower blood pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors like lisinopril inhibit the angiotensin-converting enzyme, which prevents the conversion of angiotensin I to the potent vasoconstrictor angiotensin II. This reduction in angiotensin II levels leads to decreased systemic vascular resistance and promotes sodium and water excretion via aldosterone suppression. Consequently, these effects collectively cause vasodilation and volume contraction, effectively lowering blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
