    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, I think I follow the logic of the hormonal signaling. To wrap my head around the \"blocking\" part, can you explain the actual molecular interaction at the enzyme level? Like, how does the drug physically stay attached to the ACE enzyme to stop it from working with angiotensin I?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors like lisinopril are irreversible covalent binders that form a stable acyl-enzyme complex with the heme-bound zinc ion within the enzyme's active site. Once attached, this permanent inhibition physically blocks the necessary position for zinc to facilitate the peptidic cleavage of angiotensin I, preventing the formation of angiotensin II. Because the bond is so strong, the enzyme is held in an inactive state for as long as the drug remains bound, which is why the pharmacological effect can persist for weeks after the medication is discontinued.",
      "source": "target",
